**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

Three sentences are caller context the caller already states: the zeroed-once buffer (self_attention.hpp:1553-1556, the resize() comment), one amx_qpack_slot serving either layout (self_attention.hpp:256-263), and k_vnni::layout_on picking the packer (self_attention.hpp:1700-1711). What stays is the contract, the padding precondition and the buffer size and alignment.

Proposed:
```cpp
// Pack one query group as the A operand of qk_vnni_128x4: the 4 heads' 128-dim
// bf16 rows copied as stored into rows 0..3 of `padded`. Rows 4..15 are zero
// padding the caller keeps zero: this call writes rows 0..3 only. `padded`
// holds Q_PACK_ELEMS_2048 bf16 (4096 bytes), aligned to 64 bytes.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 -> q_group, the memcpy source (amx_attn.cpp:228); line 2 -> the memcpy destination and size GROUP_HEADS_4 * HEAD_SIZE_128 (rows 0..3); line 3 -> the body being that one memcpy (rows 4..15 are never written); line 4 -> the `padded` parameter

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

'The padded Q rows are the A operand. The page's 16 K panels are the B operand. There is no transpose.' restates the new Note [QK orientation] paragraph (lines 222-227), and 'The 16-row tile stores write to a buffer inside the kernel ... `s` therefore needs 4 rows' restates Note [Tile stores write 16 rows] (lines 107-111) plus the s[4][...] shape already given. '64 tokens x 128 dims, 16 KiB' and 'so that a native K view, a packed V plane or a bare pointer cannot be handed in by mistake' restate the k_vnni_view<const bf16, PAGE_TOKENS_64, HEAD_SIZE_128> parameter type; the bit-identity guarantee is the one fact that is new and stays.

Proposed:
```cpp
// Dense QK for one page whose K is stored in the VNNI layout: computes
// S = Q . K^T with no transpose (Note [QK orientation]); writes
// s[4][s_stride_floats] fp32, all 64 token columns, UNSCALED. k_page = the
// page's packed K plane (layout in h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp); q_rows = the
// pack_q_rows_128x4 output. The scores are bit-identical to qk_rowmajor_128x4's
// (the same four 32-dim partial sums in the same order; t_amx_numerics checks).
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
lines 1-2 -> the _tile_dpbf16ps step loop with Q in Q_ROWS_TILE_4 and the K panels in tiles 5/6 (amx_attn.cpp:277-295); line 3 -> `s`, `s_stride_floats` and the 4-row memcpy (amx_attn.cpp:300-302); lines 3-4 -> the `k_page` parameter; lines 4-5 -> the `q_rows` parameter; lines 5-6 -> the four-step accumulation order of the same loop (amx_attn.cpp:277-295)

**[comments] Rule 5, a call-site comment says only why here: shorten**

The five-item list (partial or partly visible pages, hosts without AMX, the kill switch, builds without TRON_AMX_DISPATCH, shapes and query types outside the gate) is a copy of the dense-case definition and safety-chain items 1 to 5 of Note [AMX attention dispatch] (amx_attn_iface.hpp:19-55), and the same sentence already points at that Note. Keep the one fact the Note does not state for this file (this reader takes every page qk_vnni_128x4 does not) plus the pointer; lines 9-10 are only reflowed, their text is unchanged.

Proposed:
```cpp
// The AVX-512 (the CPU's 512-bit vector instruction set) QK score reader of the
// K VNNI layout (h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp). It serves every page the AMX
// (Advanced Matrix Extensions, the tile instruction set) kernel qk_vnni_128x4
// does not take (Note [AMX attention dispatch] in amx_attn_iface.hpp). No AMX
// instruction runs here. So any translation unit may include it. The layout
// itself, who writes it and who reads it are described in Note [K VNNI storage]
// (in kv_cache.hpp).
```

Let's settle to:
```
// The AVX-512 QK score reader of the K VNNI layout (h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp). 
// It serves every page the AMX kernel qk_vnni_128x4
// does not take (Note [AMX attention dispatch] in amx_attn_iface.hpp). No AMX
// instruction runs here. So any translation unit may include it. The layout
// itself, who writes it and who reads it are described in Note [K VNNI storage]
// (in kv_cache.hpp).
```

**[comments] Rule 5, a call-site comment says only why here: shorten**

The list of kernels allowed to take the plane address is owned by the callee's comment on detail::k_vnni_access (tensor/k_vnni.hpp:119-122) and by the header of that file (tensor/k_vnni.hpp:32-35); naming the other kernel here is a third copy that goes stale when a kernel is added or removed. Keep the placement fact (this function may call k_vnni_access::plane at line 86) and drop the inventory; lines 51-52 stay as they are.

Proposed:
```cpp
// k_page is the page's packed K plane as its typed view (page::k_packed). This
// reader is allowed to take the plane address (detail::k_vnni_access). q points
// at kv_mul rows of 128 values (the group's heads, row stride 128). kv_mul is
// any group size: a group of more than 16 heads is
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 46 (k_page is the typed view) -> line 62 k_page parameter; line 47 (allowed to take the plane address) -> line 86 tron::detail::k_vnni_access::plane(k_page); lines 47-48 (q points at kv_mul rows of 128, stride 128) -> line 63 q parameter and lines 105/118 q + h * HEAD_SIZE_128 + d; lines 49-50 (kv_mul any size, >16 split) -> lines 68-73 the kv_mul > MAX_KV_MUL_16 branch

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

The changed sentence (lines 1988-1990, 'the argument type of the layout-specific score kernels (the AMX QK kernel qk_vnni_128x4 reads it in place as its B operand, and the AVX-512 reader k_vnni::qk_group reads it one token per lane)') is caller context above a one-expression accessor and maps to no code line. Note [K VNNI storage] items 2 and 3 (lines 683-691) and its Why paragraph (lines 672-673) already say who reads the view and that the AMX kernel reads it as its B operand, so the sentence is cut and the colon on context line 1987 becomes a period.

Proposed:
```cpp
  // This page's packed K plane for one KV head of one slot, read-only. VNNI
  // slots only (Note [K VNNI storage]). Internal cache writes take writable views
  // from the block owner instead.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
'This page's packed K plane for one KV head of one slot, read-only' -> return type k_vnni_view<const bf16, page_size, geometry.head_size> and the body kv_block<geometry>(slot, kv_head).k.as_view(); 'VNNI slots only (Note [K VNNI storage])' -> requires clause k_vnni::layout_on<geometry.head_size>; 'Internal cache writes take writable views from the block owner instead' -> the const qualifier (no non-const overload) and .k, the owner

**[comments] Rule 4, comment lines that map to no code line: shorten**

The per-layout qualifiers (for row-major K, for VNNI K) and the Note [K VNNI storage] pointer tell the packers' story, which pack_q_rows_128x4's own comment already carries (the K layout picks the packer, one slot serves either layout). The slot comment needs only the size, the alignment and the names of the two writers.

Proposed:
```cpp
    // One packed query group for the AMX attention path (the output of
    // pack_q_group_128x4 or of pack_q_rows_128x4): 4096 bytes. alignas(64) meets
    // both packers' 64-byte alignment contract; std::vector routes over-aligned
    // elements through the aligned operator new.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
lines 1-2 (one packed group, output of either packer, 4096 bytes) -> bf16 v[amx_attn_h128g4::Q_PACK_ELEMS_2048] (262); lines 2-3 (alignas(64) meets both packers' 64-byte contract) -> struct alignas(64) amx_qpack_slot (261); lines 3-4 (std::vector routes over-aligned elements through the aligned operator new) -> std::vector<amx_qpack_slot> amx_qpack (286)

**[comments] Rule 5, a call-site comment says only why here: shorten**

The new VNNI sentence (the argument is the page's packed K plane, a k_vnni_view from page::k_packed, the B operand of qk_vnni_128x4) restates the call pg.template k_packed<...>(slot, kv_head) and qk_vnni_128x4's own comment, and the A and B operand roles added to both sentences belong to Note [QK orientation] and the kernel comments. The row-major sentence stays because it carries the pre-existing fact that this call is the only AMX dependence on consecutive K rows.

Proposed:
```cpp
      // QK for the whole page in one kernel call. The kernel writes the raw
      // scores, not yet multiplied by the attention scale, into
      // s_pages[head][token]. That is the same array and layout the software
      // loop fills. It reads K directly from storage, in the slot's layout
      // (Note [K VNNI storage] in kv_cache.hpp). In the row-major layout the
      // argument is this (slot, kv_head)'s whole K plane, a const_native_k_view
      // of 64 consecutive 128-dim rows. That call is the only place where the
      // AMX path depends on K being stored as consecutive rows. The view types
      // state the layout at the call.
```

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

The A, B and C sentences restate what names and asserts already say: 32 dimensions per step is DIM_STEP_32, the 256-byte row stride is BF16_ROW_BYTES, the panel geometry and the 64-byte load stride are the static_assert messages at lines 261-268, and the four 16-token C tiles are ACCUMULATOR_TILE_0..3 stored at s16 + b * BLOCK_TOKENS_16. The VNNI expansion restates the parameter type k_vnni_view; the proposal keeps the shape of the sibling qk_rowmajor_128x4 header (contract, operand roles, what reaches the caller, one pointer).

Proposed:
```cpp
// S[16 q][64 tok] = Q . K^T in tiles (A = the padded Q rows, B = the K panels as
// stored), then rows 0..3 copied into the caller's s buffer (UNSCALED). See the
// declaration and Note [K VNNI storage] in kv_cache.hpp.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 -> the _tile_dpbf16ps calls (2nd argument Q_ROWS_TILE_4 = A, 3rd argument K_*_PANEL_TILE_5/6 = B); line 2 -> the final memcpy loop over GROUP_HEADS_4 rows; line 3 -> pointer only: the declaration holds the s buffer contract, the Note holds who writes and reads the plane (facts no name here carries).

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

Lines 11-15 restate the fake bodies (both packer fakes bump pack_calls, pack_q_rows_128x4 also bumps rows_pack_calls, both QK fakes bump qk_calls, lines 247-274), the line-63 field comment (the packers share a signature) and the line-195 REQUIRE comment (the VNNI count checks the packer choice). The surviving five lines keep the three facts the code does not state: the build-time layout switch with its Note, that the expectation list is layout-neutral, and why only the packer choice has a counter. This replaces lines 8-15 only; line 17 and the rest of the header block (lines 1-28) stay.

Proposed:
```cpp
// present. Under TRON_K_VNNI the K plane holds the VNNI (Vector Neural Network
// Instructions) layout (Note [K VNNI storage] in kv_cache.hpp) and the dispatch
// calls the VNNI packer and QK kernel. The expectations below hold for either
// layout. The QK kernels differ in their K view type and the packers do not, so
// only the packer choice needs its own count (rows_pack_calls).
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
L1-2 (TRON_K_VNNI, VNNI layout, Note) -> k_vnni::layout_on<head_size> at line 196; L2-3 (dispatch calls the VNNI packer and QK kernel) -> the fakes pack_q_rows_128x4 (line 260) and qk_vnni_128x4 (line 268); L3-4 (expectations hold for either layout) -> the expectation list at lines 16-26; L4-5 (QK kernels differ in K view type, only the packer choice needs a count) -> rows_pack_calls (line 63), REQUIRE at line 196, and the const_native_k_view / k_vnni_view parameters at lines 253 and 268.

**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

The SECTION names below (lines 131 and 151) already name pack_q_group_128x4 and pack_q_rows_128x4, so the first clause restates identifiers; the fact that stays is that no AMX instruction runs, which is why this test has no available() guard. The PR's edit also left line 124 as a 28-character orphan ('on every host. Distinct bit') that clang-format never joins, so the proposal reflows the block with the two unchanged sentences (lines 125-126) kept verbatim.

Proposed:
```cpp
  // Both packers are scalar copies, no AMX instruction runs, so this runs on
  // every host. Distinct bit patterns per element, so a wrong index cannot pass
  // by coincidence; the comparison is on the bit patterns, not on float equality.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 (scalar copies, runs on every host) -> the test body starts at 127 with no available() check; line 2 (distinct bit patterns) -> q[i] = from_bits(0x3f80u + i) at 129; line 3 (comparison on bit patterns) -> the to_bits() REQUIREs at 141-145 and 164
